# ---------------------------------------------------------------- SECRETS: never commit
# The FortyGuard API key is read from .env at runtime. .env is NEVER committed.
# Copy .env.example to .env and paste the key there. See README.md.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
secrets*
credentials*

# A credential has already leaked into this project once, in two places, caught by a pre-commit
# scan before the first commit. FortyGuard's own /v1/heat_intelligence endpoint ALSO returns the
# caller's key inside a download URL, so any file that stores a raw API response can contain it.
# Treat every cached response as credential-bearing until scanned.
data/raw_api/
*_submit.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- large / generated
*.npy
*.npz
demo/build/
docs/_build/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- THE LIVE CACHE
# `src/live.py` caches each fetched forecast window here. It is a RUNTIME CACHE, not an artefact:
# every entry is ~2.5 MB of one hour's field, it is superseded within the hour, and N-55 makes a
# refetch byte-identical anyway, so nothing is lost by not shipping it. The saved fixtures in
# `testing/results/fixtures/` are the evidence copies that DO ship.
data/live_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BROWSER-TEST DRIVERS
# Four harnesses drive the real page by writing `demo/index.html` PLUS a driver script to a
# temporary sibling and pointing Chrome at that -- `verify_site_panels.py`, `verify_map_hover.py`,
# `verify_agent_link.py` and the screenshot helpers. They are copies of the page, they are rewritten
# from scratch on every run (never a cached copy -- gotcha #102), and each harness deletes its own
# when it finishes.
#
# This rule is for the run that DOES NOT finish. A harness killed by a timeout or a Ctrl-C leaves
# its driver behind, and a leftover copy of index.html is exactly the kind of file that gets
# committed by a `git add -A` and then read by someone as a second, diverging version of the page.
# `audit.py`'s CSS-comment check already scans every .html in this folder, so a stray one is not
# silent -- but not-committed is better than caught later.
demo/_*.html
